## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of distributed cache invalidation, cross-region consistency, and practical patterns beyond basics.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-aside pattern with per-entity versioning
- Redis Pub/Sub for cross-instance invalidations
- Key design: data and version keys (e.g., user:123:data, user:123:ver)
- TTL choice vs coherence guarantees in multi-region setups

## Code Example

```javascript
// Invalidation on write
async function invalidateUserCache(userId){
  await redis.incr(`user:${userId}:ver`);
  await redis.del(`user:${userId}:data`);
  await redis.publish('cache_invalidation', JSON.stringify({type:'user', id:userId}));
}

// Refresh on read
async function getUserProfile(userId){
  const ver = await redis.get(`user:${userId}:ver`);
  let data = await redis.get(`user:${userId}:data`);
  if(!data){
    data = await db.queryUserProfile(userId);
    await redis.set(`user:${userId}:data`, JSON.stringify(data));
  }
  // if ver changed happened elsewhere, reload data
  const currentVer = await redis.get(`user:${userId}:ver`);
  if(currentVer !== ver){
    data = await db.queryUserProfile(userId);
    await redis.set(`user:${userId}:data`, JSON.stringify(data));
  }
  return JSON.parse(data);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle batched invalidations for related entities (roles, permissions)?
- How do you detect and mitigate cache stampede during invalidations?
- What metrics would you monitor to balance freshness vs latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-protocol data governance: masking at the boundary across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC, driven by per-tenant OpenAPI contracts. It tests how to derive and enforce field visibility, roll out changes safely, and audit policy drift.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant field-level visibility across protocols
- Contract-driven masking rules derived from OpenAPI
- Central registry (Redis) for tenant/resource visibility
- Canary rollouts and rollout safety across services

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskVisible(obj, tenantId, contract) {
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(v => maskVisible(v, tenantId, contract));
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  const out = {};
  const allowed = contract?.visibleFields || [];
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
    out[k] = allowed.includes(k) ? v : 'REDACTED';
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift and automated rollback when masking changes break clients?
- What metrics would you collect to detect masking-related regressions across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?

